# CLAUDE.md (project-specific facts)

> This is a **template**. Copy it to your project root as `CLAUDE.md` (without `.example`) and fill in the placeholders. The mobile-tc-harness skill will read it during TC generation to ground the output in your specific project.
>
> **Do not commit this file with real secrets.** Use only public, non-sensitive project facts.

---

## Project identity

- **Name**: <YourProjectName>
- **Type**: <Unity mobile app / native Android / React Native / etc.>
- **Stage**: <new build / mature product / in maintenance>
- **Team size**: <solo / small / medium / large>

## Tech stack

- **Engine**: Unity <version, e.g., 2022.3.20f1 LTS>
- **Render pipeline**: <Built-in / URP / HDRP>
- **Main async library**: <UniTask / Coroutines / .NET Task>
- **DI / architecture**: <Zenject / VContainer / none>
- **Data format**: <ScriptableObject / JSON / addressables>
- **Networking**: <REST / gRPC / custom socket>
- **Backend**: <internal name or generic description>

## Android build

- **Build command**: <e.g., `./build_android.sh` or `unity -batchmode -executeMethod BuildPipeline.BuildAndroid`>
- **Output type**: <APK / AAB>
- **Min SDK**: <e.g., 24>
- **Target SDK**: <e.g., 34>
- **Primary test device**: <model name>
- **Low-end test device**: <model name — defines our "저사양" baseline>
- **Required permissions in manifest**: <list relevant ones>

## iOS build (if applicable)

- **Build command**: <e.g., `./build_ios.sh`>
- **Export method**: <development / ad-hoc / app-store>
- **Signing assumptions**: <team ID, profile name, automated or manual>
- **Primary test device**: <model name>
- **iOS version range supported**: <e.g., iOS 15.0+>

## Folder structure (top-level only)

```
<project-root>/
├── Assets/
│   ├── Scripts/              ← C# code
│   ├── UI/                   ← prefabs, scenes
│   ├── Data/                 ← ScriptableObjects, JSON
│   └── ...
├── ProjectSettings/
└── ...
```

## QA policy

- **Build Gates** (must pass before any release):
  - <e.g., Android build + APK install + first-launch smoke>
- **Smoke test path**: <one-paragraph description of "the critical user flow">
- **Regression priorities**:
  - P0 = <define: e.g., crash, data loss, payment failure>
  - P1 = <define>
  - P2 = <define>
  - P3 = <define>
- **TC tracking**: <Notion DB name / TestRail / spreadsheet / etc.>

## Notion workspace (if using Notion MCP)

- **Patch note page pattern**: <URL pattern or page naming convention — but DO NOT paste real URLs here, just the pattern>
- **Developer note page pattern**: <same>
- **TC database**: <name only, not URL>
- **TC database property mapping** (so the skill knows your schema):
  - TC ID → property "<your-name>"
  - Platform → property "<your-name>"
  - Type → property "<your-name>"
  - Priority → property "<your-name>"
  - Status → property "<your-name>"
  - (etc.)

## Conventions

- **Test method naming**: <e.g., `should_<expectedBehavior>_when_<condition>`>
- **Branch naming**: <e.g., `feature/<jira-id>-<desc>`, `fix/<id>`>
- **Commit format**: <e.g., conventional commits, custom format, free-form>
- **TC priority assignment rule**: <how you decide P0 vs P1 — write the rule down so the skill applies it consistently>

## Known constraints / quirks

- <Specific things the skill should be aware of — e.g., "always test on Wi-Fi AND LTE because our auth has different behavior", "season-end logic has a known race condition, always include a TC for it">

---

## What NOT to put in this file

- Real account IDs, API keys, signing keys, keystore paths
- Real Notion URLs / IDs
- Customer names or PII
- Internal-only URLs
- Anything that wouldn't survive being read by an external auditor
